{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>Swear to me, and he swore to him.</b> Jacob did not suspect his righteous and beloved son of transgressing his father's command or of violating the promise he had made when he said, \"I will do as you have spoken.\" Rather, he did this to strengthen the matter in Pharaoh's eyes — perhaps Pharaoh would not grant him permission to part from him, and would say to him, \"Send your brothers and your servants and let them bring him up there,\" or perhaps Pharaoh would desire that the prophet be buried in his land for their honor and merit. Therefore he had him swear, so that it would not be proper for Pharaoh to make him violate his oath, and also so that Joseph would need to strive harder in the matter because of the oath. And so it was, as he said (Genesis 50:6), \"Go up and bury your father, as you were made to swear.\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:46:11.088819578Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}